// THE ONE GROWABLE ARRAY (D-209, 1.4.7).
//
// `src/` grew 44 hand-written doubling sites over 89 growable arrays, each
// one the same four lines — double the capacity, `ralloc` the block,
// reinterpret the bytes back to the element type, store the new capacity —
// and each one carrying its own `=>!`. That cast is the reason this module
// exists rather than being a tidiness exercise: 1.4.2 found FIVE latent
// unchecked pointer reinterpretations in exactly this shape, invisible for as
// long as the callee had no type to check them against. Ninety of them
// hand-written is ninety chances to get one wrong; one of them, generic over
// the element type, is checked once.
//
// The shape is `generic_list.npk`'s, which 1.0.7 wrote to prove precisely this
// conversion ahead of time — a `wild T->` block, a count and a capacity, grown
// by doubling through `#size_of<T>()`.
//
// WILD, DELIBERATELY. The compiler allocates arena-style and frees almost
// nothing individually; every family this replaces was already `wild`, and
// making the storage managed here would change when the compiler's own tables
// die, which is a different decision from this one. The regime is unchanged by
// design.
//
// `int64` COUNT AND CAPACITY, uniformly. The families being replaced disagreed
// — `TokenList` and `Sink` counted in `int64`, the other eight in `int32` —
// and one collection type answering "how many" two ways is the thing the
// blueprint philosophy exists to prevent. `int64` is the safe side of that
// choice as well: under D-210 a doubling that overflows TRAPS, and at 64 bits
// the doubling cannot reach the trap.
//
// THE INDEX TYPE FOLLOWS THE COUNT (D-226). A family that hands its count out
// as an `int32` id keeps that width only where an external contract pins it --
// every AST node storing a name or a node reference as an `int32` is such a
// contract -- and then the narrow is GUARDED and reuses an error the module
// already declares. Where the `int32` was only an artifact of the old
// representation, the index widens with the count and nothing narrows. A newly
// declared error is the last resort: it is the one option whose cost lands on
// every `failsafe` in the tree, because REACH-002 is exhaustive over what can
// reach one. A silent `=>!` is never an answer -- two entities aliasing one id
// is the failure these tables exist to make impossible.

// STORAGE ONLY. There is no generic `list_at` returning `T`, because for an
// owning `T` that is a copy and copies of owning values are refused
// (TYPE-046). Reading an element is the family's business — `RootList` hands
// back a deep view, others read a scalar directly through `l.items[i]` — and a
// generic accessor could only serve one of those. What is generic here is the
// part that was identical everywhere and dangerous nowhere else.

// The block is `wild`, so nothing here allocates a managed body and nothing
// here drops. `alloc`/`ralloc` trap on exhaustion (HeapOom -> failsafe), which
// is why every function below is honestly `never fails`.

mod:list;
pub struct:List<T> = {
    wild T->:items;
    int64:count;
    int64:cap;
};

// A capacity of zero would make the doubling in `list_reserve` a no-op
// forever, so the floor is one. Callers asking for a specific starting size
// still get it.
pub func:list_init<T> = List<T>(int64:cap) never fails {
    int64:n = cap;
    if (n < 1i64) { n = 1i64; }
    wild int8->:mem = alloc(n * #size_of<T>());
    pass List{ items: mem =>! wild T->, count: 0i64, cap: n };
};

// Room for `need` MORE elements beyond the current count. The count is a
// parameter rather than a fixed one because the families that append in bulk
// need it — `Sink` grows by however many bytes the write is — and a
// one-element `reserve` would silently under-allocate for them. It is also
// why this doubles in a LOOP: one doubling of a capacity-8 list does not hold
// a bulk append of ten thousand, and the version that only doubled once would
// have been correct for exactly the families that never exercised it.
pub func:list_reserve<T> = NIL(List<T>->:l, int64:need) never fails {
    int64:want = l.count + need;
    if (want <= l.cap) { pass NIL; }
    int64:nc = l.cap;
    while (nc < want) { nc = nc * 2i64; }
    wild int8->:bigger = ralloc(l.items =>! wild int8->, nc * #size_of<T>());
    l.items = bigger =>! wild T->;
    l.cap = nc;
    pass NIL;
};

// `move T:v` — the parameter is consuming (D-208), which is what an OWNING
// element needs and what a scalar one is indifferent to. Both instantiations
// are exercised: `List<Root>` carries two strings per element, `List<int32>`
// carries no ownership at all.
pub func:list_push<T> = NIL(List<T>->:l, move T:v) never fails {
    drop list_reserve(l, 1i64);
    l.items[l.count] = move(v);
    l.count = l.count + 1i64;
    pass NIL;
};
